  "post": {
    "caption": "A ring once served as a signature. A signet ring has a design carved into its face, and pressed into soft wax it leaves a raised print, or seal. The design is carved in reverse so the print reads correctly. Mesopotamians rolled cylinder seals on clay over 5,000 years ago, Egyptians used scarab seals about 4,000 years ago, and about 2,000 years ago Romans used signet rings to seal letters. In medieval England a seal often stood in for a signature, and deeds were not usually signed until the 1500s. Tough stones like carnelian were popular for seal faces. Each new pope still receives a signet ring called the Fisherman's Ring.",
